<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Required by VRC.Quest.Packaging.4 — "your application must provide a valid network security
  configuration in your application manifest". The page says nothing more than that: it defines
  neither *valid* nor any position on cleartext, and no Meta page found on 2026-09-21 rules on a
  permissive base-config. So this file states, explicitly, the behaviour the platform already had
  under `usesCleartextTraffic="true"`. It loosens nothing.

  Why cleartext stays permitted (`DEC-0005`, and C-17's reasoning, unchanged): a whisper-server on
  the person's own network has no certificate, and a `domain-config` cannot express an IP RANGE.
  Allow-listing by domain would therefore refuse every RFC1918 address — the one case this
  permission exists for. The decision about WHERE cleartext may go is made in code, by
  `NetworkPolicy.requireReachable`: `https` anywhere, plain `http` only to loopback, RFC1918,
  link-local and their IPv6 equivalents. That check is called at every remote entry point.

  System trust anchors are named rather than inherited so the file says what it trusts. No user
  anchors: this app never asks a person to install a certificate, and a config that quietly
  trusted the user store would make a headset with a corporate MITM profile indistinguishable
  from a clean one.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true">
        <trust-anchors>
            <certificates src="system" />
        </trust-anchors>
    </base-config>
</network-security-config>
